How would you update stock prices in real time without refreshing the page?

Problem Statement:
A stock trading application displays live stock prices.

For example:
₹1,250 → ₹1,251 → ₹1,249 → ₹1,252

The price changes continuously, and users expect the application to update the price instantly without refreshing the screen.

Answer:
use WebSockets for real-time price updates. The client establishes a persistent WebSocket connection with the server. 
When the stock price changes, the server pushes the latest price through the existing connection, 
so the client can update the UI immediately without polling or refreshing the page.


1. Polling:

One simple approach is Polling.

The client periodically sends a request to the server:

Client → Server: Has the price changed?
Server → Client: No

Wait...

Client → Server: Has the price changed?
Server → Client: No

Wait...

Client → Server: Has the price changed?
Server → Client: Yes, ₹1,251

Problem with Polling:  
Most requests may happen when nothing has changed.

For example, if the application checks every 2 seconds:

Request → No change
Request → No change
Request → No change
Request → No change
Request → Price changed

This creates unnecessary network traffic and server load.

The problem becomes much bigger when millions of users are polling continuously.

2. WebSockets:
WebSocket provides a better approach for real-time communication.

Instead of repeatedly asking the server, the client establishes a persistent connection with the server.

Client ⇄ Server
       │
       │ Persistent Connection
       │
       ▼
   Connection stays open


Once the connection is established, both client and server can send data whenever required.

When the stock price changes, the server does not have to wait for the client to ask.

It can immediately push the new price:

Server → Client: ₹1,250
Server → Client: ₹1,251
Server → Client: ₹1,249
Server → Client: ₹1,252

The UI can then update the displayed price immediately.

3. Why WebSockets Are Useful:
WebSockets are useful when an application needs continuous, real-time updates.

Common use cases include:
Stock trading applications
Chat applications
Multiplayer games
Live location tracking
Real-time dashboards
Live notifications
Real-time data displays
